3. Complete Resource System

1. Volatile Bytes (volatileBytes)

  - Role: Immediate, unbanked currency earned via active processing or
    background daemons.
  - Storage Location: Exists purely inside volatile RAM.
  - Failure State: If Heat hits 100.0°C, volatileBytes is set to 0.
  - Cap: Mathematically capped by available RAM capacity.

2. Banked Bytes (bankedBytes)

  - Role: Persistent, secure currency.
  - Source: Transferred exclusively from volatileBytes via the Flush Cache
    action.
  - Usage: Used to purchase hardware upgrades, unlock OS levels, and purchase
    daemon scripts.
  - Persistence: Saved directly to the Roblox DataStore. Never lost on crash or
    reboot.

3. Heat (heat)

  - Role: Primary risk engine. Measured in degrees Celsius (°C).
  - Bounds: Absolute minimum = 25.0°C (Ambient). Critical failure threshold =
    100.0°C.
  - State Bands:
      - 25.0°C – 69.9°C: Nominal Band (Multiplier: 1.0x).
      - 70.0°C – 84.9°C: Warning Band (Multiplier: 1.25x; amber UI, fan audio
        initiates).
      - 85.0°C – 99.9°C: Thermal Surge Band (Multiplier: 3.0x to 10.0x dynamic;
        red UI pulse, high-pitch audio).
      - 100.0°C: BSOD State (Fatal crash).

4. RAM (ramUsed / ramMax)

  - Role: Spatial bottleneck. Measured in Megabytes (MB) up to Terabytes (TB)
    late-game.
  - Mechanic: Every processed cycle writes memory blocks into RAM.
  - Failure State: At 100% capacity, the system triggers a Buffer Stall. The
    Process button is disabled, active daemons idle, and the player cannot
    generate bytes until a Flush is performed.

5. XP & OS Levels (level, currentXP, requiredXP)

  - Role: Macro-progression metric.
  - Source: Earned strictly at a 1:1 ratio with Banked Bytes upon successful
    flushes. Lost volatile bytes award 0 XP.
  - Function: Unlocks new hardware tiers in the Shop, opens daemon slots, and
    gates prestige tiers.

6. Quantum Bits / Q-Bits (qBits - Prestige Currency)

  - Role: Non-inflationary meta-currency awarded upon performing a Kernel Reset.
  - Formula Source: Calculated based on cumulative Lifetime Banked Bytes at time
    of reset.
  - Usage: Spent in the Root Architecture Tree for permanent, run-agnostic
    multipliers (e.g., base cooling efficiency, starting hardware specs,
    automated flush logic).

4. Mathematical Formulas & Engine Logic

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| GLOBAL SIMULATION CONSTANTS                                                                       |
| Ambient Temperature (T_amb)  : 25.0°C                                                             |
| Critical Thermal Cap (T_crit): 100.0°C                                                            |
| Surge Threshold (T_surge)    : 85.0°C                                                             |
| Tick Frequency (dt)          : 0.1 seconds (10 Hz simulation step)                                |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

1. Byte Generation Formula

When a process cycle executes:

\text{BytesGenerated} = \text{Round}\left( B_{\text{base}} \times M_{\text{thermal}} \times (1 + P_{\text{qbit}}) \right)

Where:

  - B_{\text{base}} = \text{CPU Level} \times \text{Architecture Multiplier}
  - M_{\text{thermal}} = \begin{cases} 
    1.0 & \text{if } T < 70.0 \\ 
    1.25 & \text{if } 70.0 \le T < 85.0 \\ 
    3.0 + 7.0 \times \left( \frac{T - 85.0}{15.0} \right)^2 & \text{if } 85.0 \le T < 100.0 
    \end{cases}
  - P_{\text{qbit}} = \text{Permanent Prestige Multiplier Percentage}

Thermal Surge Scaling Detail: At 85.0°C, the multiplier is exactly 3.0x.
At 99.9°C, it reaches 10.0x. The quadratic curve (\frac{T-85}{15})^2 ensures the
steepest reward spike occurs in the final 2 degrees (98.0°C–99.9°C).

2. Heat Generation Formula

Every process cycle adds an instantaneous thermal impulse:

\Delta T_{\text{add}} = H_{\text{base}} \times \left( 1 + \frac{\text{CPU Level}}{8} \right) \times \left( 1 - C_{\text{paste}} \right)

Where:

  - H_{\text{base}} = 2.50^\circ\text{C}
  - C_{\text{paste}} = \text{Thermal Compound efficiency percentage (0.0 to 0.40)}

3. Cooling Dissipation Formula

Evaluated every simulation tick (dt = 0.1\text{s}):

T_{t+dt} = \max\left( T_{\text{amb}}, T_t - \left( R_{\text{cool}} \times \left[ 1 + 0.5 \times \left( \frac{T_t - T_{\text{amb}}}{T_{\text{crit}} - T_{\text{amb}}} \right) \right] \times dt \right) \right)

  - Newton's Law of Cooling approximation: Cooling efficiency naturally scales
    up by up to 50% as the differential between system heat and ambient heat
    widens.
  - R_{\text{cool}} = \text{Sum of all active Fans, Heat Sinks, and Liquid Radiators (in } ^\circ\text{C/sec)}.

4. RAM Allocation & Saturation Formula

Each processed cycle consumes volatile memory:

\text{RAM}_{\text{cost}} = \text{RAM}_{\text{base}} \times \left( 1 + \frac{\text{BytesGenerated}}{500} \right)

  - At Level 1: \text{RAM}_{\text{cost}} = 2.0\text{ MB}.
  - When \text{ramUsed} + \text{RAM}_{\text{cost}} > \text{ramMax}, generation
    is aborted and state flag BUFFER_STALL = true is set.

5. Flush Payout & Time Formula

When the Flush sequence completes:

\text{BankedPayout} = \text{Round}\left( \text{volatileBytes} \times \left( 1 + \text{BusBonus} \right) \right)

The channel duration required to flush:

\text{Duration}_{\text{flush}} = \max\left( 0.25, 1.50 \times \left( \frac{\text{ramUsed}}{\text{ramMax}} \right) \times (1 - \text{BusSpeedReduction}) \right)

6. Crash Execution & Penalties

Triggered the instant T \ge 100.0^\circ\text{C}:

\begin{aligned} \text{volatileBytes} &\leftarrow 0 \ \text{ramUsed}
&\leftarrow 0 \ \text{heat} &\leftarrow T_{\text{amb}} \quad (\text{upon reboot
completion}) \ \text{InputLocked} &\leftarrow \text{true} \quad (\text{Duration:
} 5.0\text{ seconds}) \end{aligned}

